H2: What Is an MT5 Grey Label?
The easiest way to understand a grey label is to place it between two other models.
At one end, there's the introducing broker. An IB refers clients to someone else's brokerage and earns a share of the revenue. The clients belong to the other broker, and so does the brand.
At the other end, there's the white label. The broker runs a branded MT5 environment with broad control over configuration, trading conditions and how the platform is managed.
A forex grey label sits in the middle. You run your own branded brokerage and manage your own clients, but you do it within another provider's MT5 infrastructure and under that provider's licensing arrangement. You're given your own space inside an existing environment, configured with your branding, your account types and your trading conditions, within the limits the host arrangement allows.
For a lot of new brokers, that middle ground is the right place to start. It's a real brokerage with a real brand, not a referral scheme, but without the heavier commitment a white label requires.
One thing to be clear about from the start: a grey label is a technology and infrastructure arrangement. It isn't a regulatory shortcut. Whether you need a registered company, a licence or other approvals depends on your jurisdiction, your business model and the specific provider arrangement. You'll want qualified legal advice on that side.
H2: How It Works
Think of the host provider's MT5 environment as a building, and your grey label as a fully fitted office inside it. The building's foundations, power and security are managed by the host. Your office has your name on the door, your own layout and your own clients coming through it.
In practical terms, the setup works like this.
Your brokerage is allocated its own section within the host MT5 environment. Inside that section, your trading groups and account types are created, your symbols and trading conditions are configured, and your branding is applied to the client-facing experience.
A forex CRM is then connected to your section of the platform. This is where your team works day to day: managing leads, verifying clients, processing deposits and withdrawals, opening trading accounts and tracking IBs.
Your website and client portal sit in front of all of this. A trader finds your brokerage, registers, completes verification, funds their account and starts trading on MT5 under your brand. The underlying server infrastructure is maintained by the host, so you don't have to run servers, manage platform upgrades or handle hardware.
That's the core appeal of an MT5 grey label platform. Your clients get a professional, branded brokerage. Your team gets the tools to run it. And the heaviest infrastructure responsibilities stay with the host.
