H2: Grey Label vs White Label
This is the decision most founders spend the longest on, and it's worth getting right.
H3: Control
 A white label generally gives you greater control over the platform, its configuration, trading conditions and how the environment is managed. A grey label works within another provider's infrastructure and arrangement, so some decisions sit with the host rather than with you.
H3: Cost
 A grey label usually has a noticeably lower entry cost. You're not paying for a full platform environment or the infrastructure to support it. A white label requires a larger upfront and ongoing investment.
H3: Responsibility
 With a grey label, the host carries much of the infrastructure responsibility. With a white label, more of that responsibility moves to your side and your technology partner.
H3: Flexibility
 White labels allow deeper customisation and are better suited to brokers who want to shape every detail of their offering. Grey labels offer enough flexibility for most launch-stage brokers, but with clear limits.
H3: Dependence
 Under a grey label, your business relies more heavily on the host provider's stability, policies and decisions. That isn't necessarily a problem, but it's something to go in with eyes open.
Neither model is better across the board. A grey label is often the smarter first step, and a white label tends to make more sense once volumes and plans justify it. If you're likely to move up later, we can plan your CRM and integrations now so the switch is far less disruptive when the time comes. Our MT5 white label page covers that model in more detail.
